data: {"id":"chatcmpl_91183170-0063-4494-abaf-46fb958a3b4b","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1792096977,"model":"oracle/chrono-chatgpt-pro-pool","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"Referee findingC1 is special_case_of_known: on its conventional existential reading, it is true and is a direct specialization of the finite pigeonhole principle, not an open problem. The applicable general statement appears in the opened MIT source as Rule 11.10.1, §11.10, printed page 343 (PDF page 31). MIT OpenCourseWareNo PDF is attached in the current conversation; the file tool reported no conversation files. This report therefore evaluates the supplied C1 text and diagnostic excerpts, not an unseen manuscript.Well-posedness and quantifiersThe intended statement is:\n$$\\begin{gathered}\n\\text{For every finite }A,B,C\\text{ with }|C|\\le |B|\u003c|A|,\\\\\n\\forall f:A\\to B,\\ \\forall g:B\\to C,\\quad\n\\exists a,a'\\in A:\\quad\na\\ne a'\\ \\land\\ g(f(a))=g(f(a')).\n\\end{gathered}$$\nHere the arrows denote total functions. The pair is existential and may depend on the maps; the statement does not say that every distinct pair has equal images. Writing “there exist distinct  $a,a'\\in A$ ” would remove the only appreciable ambiguity.No additional nonemptiness assumption is needed. Choices of sets for which one of the required functions does not exist are vacuous cases of the universal formulation, not counterexamples. With both functions actually supplied under the cardinality assumptions,  $B$  and  $C$  are nonempty and  $A$  contains at least two elements.Opened baseline and conditional proof escapeThe official Mathlib documentation independently gives Fintype.exists_ne_map_eq_of_card_lt, with finite domain and codomain, a total function, a strict cardinality inequality, and an existential conclusion asserting distinct inputs with equal outputs. This was a documentation inspection, not a Lean compilation or verification of this submission. Lean CommunityFor source comparison, C1 matches the general pigeonhole statement under the substitution\n$$(X,Y,h)=(A,C,g\\circ f).$$\nIts required cardinality comparison is supplied by the stated inequalities using ordinary order transitivity. Expanding the composition gives exactly the displayed conclusion. These are theorem instantiation, assumption substitution, and normalization; there is no additional mathematical obligation in C1 beyond that baseline. Consequently, the statement-level conditional escape classification is bind_only. This finding rests on an inspected applicable theorem, not merely on the absence of a submitted proof. MIT OpenCourseWareThe hypothesis audit also identifies surplus assumptions: the bound  $|C|\\le |B|$ , and even the finiteness of  $C$ , are unnecessary when the first map still has finite domain and codomain with  $|B|\u003c|A|$ . The same known baseline applied to the first map, followed by equality preservation under a function, already covers that version. Removing these assumptions would improve the formulation but would not make it an open conjecture. Lean CommunitySeparate assessmentsCorrectness: not_checked refers to the submitted proof, because none was supplied. It does not contradict the affirmative literature-supported status of the assertion. Proof shape is null, and there are no live proof witnesses to report.Utility: limited, inherited general_theorem utility is supported: C1 expresses a universal obstruction to injectivity, not merely a bounded experiment. That does not establish a new result, computational improvement, checker, or application attributable to this submission.Information gain and settlement: no quantitative information-gain assessment is supported by the supplied material. No named open-problem settlement is claimed, so both optional objects remain null.The six supplied diagnostic excerpts were compared at the statement level. Two define other questions; the remaining excerpts concern ideal reductions, decision-tree bounds, product-law factorization, and probability-law countermodels. The decision-tree excerpt mentions a relevant generic injectivity/cardinality ingredient, but the domain-specific results themselves are not exact matches for C1. Their reported hashes, frozen status, and compilation metadata were not independently validated. These retrieval results neither establish novelty nor undermine the directly applicable external baseline.Recommendation: revise the classification and presentation. Present C1 as a known corollary or exercise, make the existential quantifier explicit, and remove or explain the redundant restrictions. Its current mathematical content does not justify display as an unresolved conjecture. This is feedback on the submitted statement, not a publication decision. MIT OpenCourseWareJSON{\n  \"recommendation\": \"revise\",\n  \"summary\": \"C1 is well-posed on the conventional existential reading and is a special case of the known finite pigeonhole principle. Its conditional statement-level escape is bind_only because the inspected baseline covers the conclusion through instantiation and normalization. No proof was submitted, so correctness is not_checked, proof shape is null, and there are no live witnesses. No attached PDF was available.\",\n  \"strengths\": [\n    \"The finite-set setting and function domains permit a precise comparison with an established general theorem.\",\n    \"The assertion has a clear general existence meaning once the existential quantifier is written explicitly.\"\n  ],\n  \"concerns\": [\n    \"The assertion is already covered by an opened pigeonhole-principle source and should not be presented as open.\",\n    \"The phrase 'distinct a,a′ satisfy' should explicitly say 'there exist distinct a,a′ in A'.\",\n    \"The cardinality bound on C and its finiteness are redundant for the conclusion when the first-map cardinality inequality is retained.\",\n    \"No attached PDF or submitted proof was available for inspection.\"\n  ],\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"C1\",\n      \"correctness\": \"not_checked\",\n      \"shape\": null,\n      \"witnesses\": [],\n      \"known\": [\n        \"6.042J Chapter 11: Cardinality rules [opened: https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-042j-mathematics-for-computer-science-fall-2010/fd14182dfb1a679eda5cbd38c48c8bb5_MIT6_042JF10_chap11.pdf]\",\n        \"Mathlib.Data.Fintype.Pigeonhole [opened: https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Data/Fintype/Pigeonhole.html]\"\n      ],\n      \"note\": \"The known-status assessment is affirmative, while proof correctness remains not_checked because this is a statement-only submission. The bind_only assessment concerns the conditional obligation posed by C1 relative to inspected prerequisites, not an unseen proof's possible methodological features. General-theorem utility is supported only as inherited known mathematics; no new computational or research advance is established. Information gain is not assessed, and no named open-problem settlement is claimed.\",\n      \"conjecture\": {\n        \"well_posed\": true,\n        \"well_posed_reason\": \"Read conventionally, the statement universally quantifies over finite A,B,C with |C|≤|B|\u003c|A|, then over total functions f:A→B and g:B→C, and existentially quantifies a,a′∈A with a≠a′ and equal composite images. The existential quantifier should be made explicit. There is no injectivity or surjectivity assumption. Empty function spaces are vacuous cases of the universal statement; no extra nonemptiness hypothesis is necessary.\",\n        \"status\": \"special_case_of_known\",\n        \"status_reason\": \"MIT Rule 11.10.1 and the documented Mathlib theorem Fintype.exists_ne_map_eq_of_card_lt give the applicable general finite pigeonhole statement. C1 requires only substitution of its composite map and routine use of its cardinality assumptions. This status records a known affirmative answer, not an unresolved question.\",\n        \"named_works\": [\n          \"6.042J Chapter 11: Cardinality rules [opened: https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-042j-mathematics-for-computer-science-fall-2010/fd14182dfb1a679eda5cbd38c48c8bb5_MIT6_042JF10_chap11.pdf]\",\n          \"Mathlib.Data.Fintype.Pigeonhole [opened: https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Data/Fintype/Pigeonhole.html]\"\n        ],\n        \"escape\": \"bind_only\",\n        \"escape_reason\": \"Relative to the inspected pigeonhole baseline, the composite is a substituted function expres"},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl_91183170-0063-4494-abaf-46fb958a3b4b","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1792096977,"model":"oracle/chrono-chatgpt-pro-pool","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"sion, not a construction carrying an additional mathematical property. Cardinality transitivity and expansion of composition complete the source match. C1 adds no non-binding obligation.\",\n        \"suggestions\": [\n          \"Write the conclusion as 'there exist distinct a,a′∈A such that g(f(a))=g(f(a′))', with the existential quantifiers after the universal function quantifiers.\",\n          \"Label the assertion as a known pigeonhole-principle corollary or exercise and cite the applicable general statement.\",\n          \"Remove the unnecessary restriction |C|≤|B| and the finiteness of C, or explain why those restrictions are retained for an expository purpose; this simplification does not create a new open problem.\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"methodology\": {\n        \"baseline\": [\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"A,B,C are finite sets; |C|≤|B| and |B|\u003c|A|; f:A→B and g:B→C are total functions.\",\n            \"location\": \"Provided manuscript text: CONFIRMED STATEMENTS[0].statement, C1, sentences 1–2; repeated in SOURCE CONTEXT.\",\n            \"source\": null\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"For finite sets X,Y and every total function h:X→Y, the hypothesis |Y|\u003c|X| guarantees distinct x,y∈X with h(x)=h(y).\",\n            \"location\": \"Section 11.10, Rule 11.10.1; printed page 343, PDF page 31. The theorem page was also visually inspected.\",\n            \"source\": \"6.042J Chapter 11: Cardinality rules [opened: https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-042j-mathematics-for-computer-science-fall-2010/fd14182dfb1a679eda5cbd38c48c8bb5_MIT6_042JF10_chap11.pdf]\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"For finite types α,β, a function h:α→β and card β \u003c card α, there exist x,y:α with x≠y and h(x)=h(y). No separate nonemptiness hypothesis appears.\",\n            \"location\": \"Fintype.exists_ne_map_eq_of_card_lt; equivalent library formulation corroborating the preceding baseline, not an additional mathematical assumption.\",\n            \"source\": \"Mathlib.Data.Fintype.Pigeonhole [opened: https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Data/Fintype/Pigeonhole.html]\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"searched\": [\n          \"Conversation-file semantic query: 'pigeonhole-composed finite sets A B C |C|≤|B|\u003c|A| maps f:A→B g:B→C distinct a a′ g(f(a))=g(f(a′))'. The tool reported that no files are present in the current conversation.\",\n          \"General web-index queries: 'site.leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs Fintype exists_ne_map_eq_of_card_lt' and 'site.math.mit.edu pigeonhole principle finite sets function injective cardinality'.\",\n          \"Opened the two sources recorded in baseline and known. Inspected the complete finite-type theorem declaration and the MIT theorem page, including its page image.\",\n          \"Compared all six supplied diagnostic excerpts at the statement level. Two are definitions of other questions. The other domains are ideal reductions, decision-tree counting, product-law factorization and probability-law countermodels. The decision-tree excerpt cites an injectivity/cardinality ingredient but does not itself state C1.\",\n          \"Scope was a bounded search for a sufficient established baseline. No exhaustive historical, MathSciNet, zbMATH or arXiv priority search was performed. The root knowledge_queries entry is a proposal for later execution, not a completed search.\"\n        ],\n        \"literature\": \"supported\",\n        \"binding_analysis\": \"The external theorem parameters match X=A, Y=C and h=(a↦g(f(a))). The supplied inequalities discharge its cardinality premise by ordinary order transitivity. Expansion of composition identifies its conclusion with C1. No new alias, helper lemma, tactic expansion, estimate, enumeration or structural construction is present in the submitted text. The first-map specialization also exposes the restrictions on C as surplus under equality preservation. This is a source-applicability comparison, not an audit of a submitted deduction.\",\n        \"delta\": \"No additional mathematical proposition remains beyond the recorded baseline for C1 as written. A proof of this exact assertion would certify an already covered existential conclusion. No additional constraint on the colliding inputs, new bound, computational guarantee or construction is requested. Any separate novelty of an unsubmitted proof method remains unevaluated.\",\n        \"escape\": \"bind_only\",\n        \"witnesses\": [],\n        \"utility\": {\n          \"kind\": \"general_theorem\",\n          \"status\": \"supported\",\n          \"rationale\": \"The assertion expresses a general obstruction to injectivity over arbitrary admissible finite sets and maps, rather than a bounded positive experiment. That mathematical utility is wholly inherited from the inspected baseline. It does not establish a valuable open conjecture, a new theorem, a checker, or a computational improvement attributable to the submission.\",\n          \"problem\": \"For finite A,B,C with |C|≤|B|\u003c|A| and total maps f:A→B and g:B→C, must there exist distinct a,a′∈A with g(f(a))=g(f(a′))?\",\n          \"progress\": \"The existence question is already answered by the opened general baseline. No additional research or computational advance is established by the statement-only submission.\",\n          \"prerequisites\": \"Finite A,B,C; total f:A→B and g:B→C; |C|≤|B|\u003c|A|; the recorded finite pigeonhole theorem.\",\n          \"discharge\": \"C1 explicitly supplies the map data and cardinality assumptions needed for the source match after composition substitution and order transitivity. This checks baseline applicability, not a submitted proof or a computed instance.\"\n        },\n        \"settlement\": null,\n        \"information_gain\": null,\n        \"limits\": [\n          \"No attached PDF was available; manuscript-local locations refer only to the supplied text.\",\n          \"The assessment uses the conventional existential reading, not a universal-pair assertion.\",\n          \"No proof was submitted: correctness is not_checked, shape is null, and no live witnesses are reported.\",\n          \"The search establishes a sufficient known baseline, not exhaustive historical priority.\",\n          \"Supplied diagnostic hashes, frozen status, axiom reports and compilation claims are not_checkable in this report; no independent material or build validation was performed.\",\n          \"Supported utility is inherited general-theorem utility only. No quantitative information gain or named open-problem settlement is established.\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"knowledge_queries\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"C1\",\n      \"definitions\": [\n        \"|X| is the number of elements of a finite set X.\",\n        \"The composition g∘f sends a∈A to g(f(a))∈C.\"\n      ],\n      \"hypotheses\": [\n        \"A,B,C are finite sets.\",\n        \"|C|≤|B|.\",\n        \"|B|\u003c|A|.\",\n        \"f:A→B is a total function.\",\n        \"g:B→C is a total function.\"\n      ],\n      \"quantifiers\": \"For every finite A,B,C satisfying the cardinality inequalities, for every f:A→B and every g:B→C, there exist a,a′∈A. The pair may depend on the maps. No nonemptiness assumption is added; nonexistent function choices give vacuous cases.\",\n      \"conclusion\": \"There exist a,a′∈A such that a≠a′ and g(f(a))=g(f(a′)).\",\n      \"search_terms\": [\n        \"finite pigeonhole principle\",\n        \"Dirichlet box principle\",\n        \"collision in a function to a smaller finite set\",\n        \"finite cardinality obstruction to injectivity\",\n        \"postcomposition preserves equal images\",\n        \"injectivity of a composite implies injectivity of the first map\",\n        \"Fintype.exists_ne_map_eq_of_card_lt\",\n        \"Fintype.card_le_of_injective\"\n      ],\n      \"reason\": \"Retrieve a generic collision or injectivity-cardinality statement with matching total-function hypotheses and degenerate cases, rather than unrelated domain-specific keyword hits. This is one proposed semantic query for later source auditing; it is not a completed query or a verification claim.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"limits\": [\n    \"The file tool reported no current-conversation files, so the requested PDF could not be "},"finish_reason":null}]}

